 Find the area of a rectangle formed by trisecting a regular octagon. </think> <answer> The area of a regular octagon can be divided into eight congruent triangles, as shown in the image below, and each of those triangles has an area of \[\frac{1}{8}A_{\square ABCDEFGH}=\frac{1}{8}\cdot 1=\frac{1}{8}.\]~[asy]
size(3cm);
draw(A--B--C--D--E--F--G--H--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
pair X=(B+H)/2;
pair Y=(B+D)/2;
pair Z=(E+A)/2;
pair W=(B+E)/2;
draw(X--Y--Z--W--cycle,linewidth(1.2));
label("$A$",A,N);
label("$B$",B,NW);
label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$D$",D,WSW);
label("$E$",E,S);
label("$F$",F,SE);
label("$G$",G,ENE);
label("$H$",H,WNW);
[/asy]Referring to the image above, we can note that $ABEF$ forms a rectangle that is spanned between three of the isosceles triangles listed above. Since the isosceles triangle take on one-third of the regular octagon, the shortest side of the rectangle we want to find has length $\frac{3}{8}$ and the longest side has length $\frac{5}{8}$ (by applying Pythagoras' Theorem to the images in the figure above). Multiplying, we find that the area of $ABEF$ is \[\frac{3}{8}\cdot\frac{5}{8}=\boxed{\frac{15}{64}}.\]
